can we override static method in abstract class
No. Static methods cannot be overridden, whether they are in an abstract class or a normal class.

Example
abstract class Animal {

    static void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal Sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    static void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog Sound");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal.sound();
        Dog.sound();
    }
}
Output
Animal Sound
Dog Sound

Notice that both methods exist independently.

This is method hiding, not overriding.

Why isn't it overriding?

Overriding is based on runtime polymorphism.

For instance methods:

Animal a = new Dog();
a.sound();   // Dog's implementation

The JVM decides at runtime which method to call.

But static methods belong to the class, not to an object.

The JVM knows exactly which method to call at compile time.

So:

Animal.sound();   // Calls Animal's static method
Dog.sound();      // Calls Dog's static method

No runtime decision is involved.

Can an abstract class have static methods?

✅ Yes.

abstract class Animal {

    static void display() {
        System.out.println("Static Method");
    }

    abstract void sound();
}

This is perfectly valid.

Can a static method be abstract?

❌ No.

This is illegal.

abstract class Animal {

    abstract static void sound();   // Compile-time error
}
Why?
abstract means the subclass must provide an implementation.
static methods belong to the class itself and cannot be overridden.

These two concepts contradict each other.

