5) how do you implement security 
we implemented security using Spring Security with JWT-based authentication. We configured Spring Security using a SecurityConfig class, where we defined public endpoints such as /login 
and protected all other APIs. During login, the user submits their username and password. Spring Security uses the AuthenticationManager and UserDetailsService to validate the 
credentials against the database. Passwords are stored in encrypted form using BCryptPasswordEncoder. If authentication is successful, we generate a JWT token and return it to the client.
For every subsequent request, the client sends the JWT token in the Authorization header using the Bearer scheme. A custom JWT filter intercepts each request, validates the token 
by checking its signature and expiration, loads the user details, and stores the authentication in the SecurityContextHolder. Once authenticated, the request proceeds to the controller.
Authorization is then enforced based on roles or authorities configured in Spring Security.
