5) solid principles 

 Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
Definition

A class should have only one responsibility or one reason to change.

❌ Bad Example
class EmployeeService {
    
    public void saveEmployee() {
        // save employee
    }

    public void generateReport() {
        // generate report
    }

    public void sendEmail() {
        // send email
    }
}

This class:

saving employee
generating report
sending email

Too many responsibilities.

✅ Good Example
class EmployeeService {
    public void saveEmployee() {}
}

class ReportService {
    public void generateReport() {}
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail() {}
}
Real-time Example (Tell in Interview)

“In Spring Boot projects, we usually separate responsibilities into Controller, Service, and Repository layers. Controller handles requests, Service contains
business logic, and Repository handles database operations. This follows Single Responsibility Principle.”

🔥 Very good interview answer.

O — Open Closed Principle (OCP)
Definition

Software should be:

open for extension
closed for modification

Meaning:
👉 new functionality add cheyyali
👉 existing code ekkuva modify cheyyakudadhu

Real-time Example

Payment methods.

❌ Bad Example
class PaymentService {

    public void pay(String type) {

        if(type.equals("UPI")) {
            // UPI payment
        }
        else if(type.equals("CARD")) {
            // Card payment
        }
    }
}

Every new payment type ki existing code modify cheyyali.

✅ Good Example
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {
    public void pay() {}
}

class CardPayment implements Payment {
    public void pay() {}
}
Interview Answer

“Using interfaces and polymorphism in Java helps us follow Open Closed Principle because we can add new implementations without modifying existing business logic.”

L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
Definition

Child class should be replaceable with parent class without breaking functionality.

❌ Bad Example
class Bird {
    void fly(){}
}

class Penguin extends Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }
}

Penguin cannot fly.
So inheritance wrong.

Interview-friendly Explanation

“Subclass should properly behave like parent class. Otherwise inheritance design becomes incorrect.”

Real-time Example

In Spring Boot:

multiple implementations of same interface
service implementations interchangeable
I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Definition

Client should not depend on methods it does not use.

❌ Bad Example
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

Robot doesn’t eat.

✅ Good Example
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}
Real-time Example

“Instead of creating one large interface with many unrelated methods, we create smaller specific interfaces.”

D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
Definition

High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules directly.
Both should depend on abstractions.

❌ Bad Example
class OrderService {
    MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase();
}

Tightly coupled.

✅ Good Example
interface Database {
    void connect();
}

class MySQLDatabase implements Database {
    public void connect(){}
}

class OrderService {

    Database db;

    OrderService(Database db){
        this.db = db;
    }
}
Real-time Spring Boot Example

🔥 MOST IMPORTANT ANSWER

“Spring Boot Dependency Injection using @Autowired is the best example of Dependency Inversion Principle because service classes depend on interfaces/abstractions instead of concrete implementations.”

🎯 Short Interview Summary
Principle	Meaning
SRP	One class → one responsibility
OCP	Extend behavior without modifying code
LSP	Child should properly replace parent
ISP	Small specific interfaces
DIP	Depend on abstraction, not implementation


